Date: Tue, 31 Aug 1993 02:10:19 -0400
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: POLI: More stupid patents...

   Just a few remarks on the "hexium" scam:
- It *is* possible to get a trademark for a product you don't yet have;
  for this, you have to have a 'bona fide' intention to deliver the
product in the near future; if you don't, you lose the trademark.
This is the way Intel trademarked Pentium (I checked the database).
If I get the trademark now, Intel might not want to delay the P6 release
until my intention expires.
  Also, it is easy to come up with a real product in the same trademark
category to [unsuccessfully] market, for which the 'Hex'/'6' name
will have some meaning (not required, but feels more natural) - say,
a 6-legged, 6-pound,  The Biggest Russian Microchip.
 
  Hexium/Septium/Octium have not been registered, I checked.
 
  Also, whatever department at Intel is responsible for naming the chips,
they haven't been doing such a great job: the first processors were
named with [untrademarkable and not that easy to memorize] numbers;
Pentium was named too late (it wouldn't have hurt Intel to have this
name in the news for another year, and let it sink, instead of P5, etc.
- better familiarity of the market with the name could bring extra millions
of dollars), the contest for it was a child's play (naming a multi-billion
dollar product deserves more attention than the last-minute, $1000-reward
employee-only contest), etc., etc.
  With that in mind, Intel's failure to secure the name-line of successors
(which is not easy, due to the expiration time for 'bona fide' intentions)
seems quite possible.
 
   I can imagine that a[n apparent] scam like this could actually legally
work.  If, however, the case was submitted to a jury that would listen to
their conscience...

