> Pentium was named too late (it wouldn't have hurt Intel to have this
> name in the news for another year, and let it sink, instead of P5, etc.
> - better familiarity of the market with the name could bring extra millions
> of dollars), the contest for it was a child's play (naming a multi-billion
> dollar product deserves more attention than the last-minute, $1000-reward
> employee-only contest), etc., etc.
>   With that in mind, Intel's failure to secure the name-line of successors
> (which is not easy, due to the expiration time for 'bona fide' intentions)
> seems quite possible.

First, if the names in question have not been registered, and it is possible
for someone else to do so in the same category, I seriously doubt Intel
intends to use them. Personally, I'd be very disappointed if the P6 were
to eventually be called "Hexium".

Second, that department which "wasn't doing such a great job" at naming
the chips, never even existed! In the past, Intel never saw a need to worry
about the names of its chips. So, the oversight was a legal one, not a
technical one. Once they recognized the need to create trademarkable names,
they embarked upon the search which ultimately ended up with Pentium.

Third, Pentium was not named too late. Had it been introduced to the market
too early, it could have been seriously weakened by misuse of the name by
competitors. In addition, I don't believe the name was even arrived at until
just weeks before it was announced. Remember, Intel was well into the final
stages of P5 development before it even realized the need to name it something
other than 586.

Fourth, the Pentium processor name did not come from an employees-only
contest. There was indeed a contest held, in the hopes of capturing a spark
of originality from our collective minds, but Pentium was not an employee
suggested name. Intel hired a number of companies which do nothing _but_
brand naming and marketing to come up with the name, and they were involved
in reviewing the myriad of suggestions, in addition to coming up with others
of their own. Pentium ultimately came from a batch of names submitted by
one of these companies, and reviewed by Intel. You say the naming deserved
"more attention". It probably _received_ more attention, and money, than
anything else you can think of, from NutraSweet to CocaCola.

So finally, your conclusion, based upon Intel's alleged failure in these
areas, that they have overlooked your trademarking scenario, is probably
highly unlikely. 

Academically, it is an interesting topic, and one which I am sure has much
relevance in the market, since it probably happens all the time, but in 
this particular case - no.

And _how_ does this relate to Extropianism? Well, we have discussed patents
and copyrights, but I'm not sure if anyone has specifically addressed
trademarks. At first they might be easily dismissed by the extropians as
having no more relevance than patents. Before doing so, however, one should
remember exactly what trademarks do for the customer:

1. Indicate what you are buying. If it says CocaCola outside, you know you're
   buying soda.

2. Indicates quality. If you've come to know that a given product delivers
   to a specific level of quality, you should expect that same quality from
   the same product in the future.

Well, there was something else, but I forgot what it was. The point is, 
imagine a marketplace where anyone could duplicate CocaColas trademarks and
logos on identical aluminum cans, and put anything they wanted in those
cans. Of course, companies try to come as close as possible to doing this
today, duplicating the _appearance_ of the packaging, or using a _very_
similar name, but only the laws keep them from going all the way.

So, as useful as trademarks and logos and brand names and such are to the
consumer, what do we do with them? The obvious drawback is the abuse of
them, wherein people might seek to trademark everything in sight, much as
lawyers are copyrighting and/or patenting all the code they can find today.
Personally, I _like_ the way the law treats trademarks and such today. If
it's unique, it is fair game. If not, you're out of luck.

Tony Hamilton
