He asks:
>If you were at the party and are reading this, can you think of 
>anything I left out?

I became a permanent fixture at the hot-tub (alas, my weakness) 
between the hours of 10 and 4, so my perspective is the *hot-tub
perspective*.

More Highlights:

* Extropians filled up the small restaurant the next morning for 
breakfast, using every chair and table available. In true 
multi-tasking fashion, at least 6 conversations were going on
at the same time. I enjoyed my conversation with Ralph Whelan
discussing Alcor's soon-to-occur massive move between Riverside
and Scottsdale.  (I still like Vince's suggestion of putting
a McDonald's logo -CAUTION! Frozen Chicken McNuggets!- on the 
trucks as they make their way slowly through the bleak terrain 
carrying dewars of suspended heads and bodies!)

* Upon annoucing that the 28th was his 34th birthday, Robin Hanson
was pleased and embarrassed to receive a rousing Happy Birthday
song, spontaneously ordered of course, by all hot tub enthusiasts. 

* Zak Zaidman is back and healthy, apparently beating his cancer. He
 was proudly showing off his battle scars of his recent surgery.

* The moon put on a nice show, being almost full and slowly arc-ing
its way across the sky, finally setting at about 3:30 by accentuating 
the trees on top of the neighboring ridge.

Some Ponderables:

* Perry Metzger is actually a nice guy.

* Max More was dressed all evening for the hot tub, but he never made
  it there.

* Tim May and Eric Raymond politely avoided each other all evening, 
  averting what many thought would be a fireworks display.

* Stig honed his effective, social, ice-breaking technique of
  giving his massage services to many of the other hot tub 
  enthusiasts, mostly of the female contingent. Do you suppose that
  the tub would have more crowded had members of the female contingent
  responded in kind by offering their massage services? 


